  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Unsettled pattern for the upcoming week with \nmultiple systems forecasted to move through the Commonwealth\n\nImproving weather conditions can be expected today as the \nremnants of Isaias exit the Mid Atlantic. Rain chances on \nTuesday appear to be maximized over the northern tier where a \nshortwave aloft and sfc cold front (infused with moisture from \nTS Rachel?) streak eastward from the Great Lakes. SPC has \nhighlighted this area with a conditional low risk level 1/5 for \nsevere t-storms. Wednesday into Thursday another cold front \nwill move through the Commonwealth bringing an additional round \nof rain showers, especially across the western half of \nPennsylvania. Then, another strong system will deliver rain\nperhaps transitioning to some high elevation snowflakes this\nweekend.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Big temperature swing forecast for the week ahead \n\nNoticeably warmer and more humid conditions expected early this\nweek with max temps on Tuesday forecast to peak 10-15 degrees \nabove the historical average for mid October and possibly\nchallenge daily records with highs in the 70-80F range. \n\nConfidence is increasing in a seasonably chilly cooldown and big\ntemperature swing (vs. early in the week) for the upcoming\nweekend. The substantial cooldown appears to be delivered in \nthe wake of a strong cold front by gusty west-northwest flow \ndirecting the colder air over the Great Lakes. This could setup\nsome lake effect/upslope rain or snow showers (yes, first flakes\npossible in the Alleghenies/Laurel Highlands) followed by frost\nand freeze conditions. To quantify the magnitude of the fcst \ntemp swing this week, Bradford could go from 75 degrees Tuesday \nafternoon to 25 degrees by early Sunday morning (50 degree \nswing!) If the current forecast verifies, Sunday morning may\nbring a widespread freeze to the Commonwealth.",
